<header class="re-art-hero"><div class="container"><a class="re-art-back" href="/insights/">INSIGHTS / REAL ESTATE</a><h1>Facebook Ads for Realtors: Campaigns, Creative and Lead Quality</h1><p>How real estate agents can approach Meta housing ads, listing creative, landing pages and CRM follow-up without treating every form as a qualified lead.</p></div></header>
<article class="re-art-body"><p class="re-art-meta">LeadsFlo Editorial · November 5, 2026</p><aside><strong>THE SHORT ANSWER</strong><p>Facebook and Instagram ads can help agents distribute relevant property and market information, but housing advertising requires compliance with Meta&#x27;s Special Ad Category rules and applicable fair housing laws. Strong results depend on the message, response process and lead quality.</p></aside><div class="re-art-cta"><strong>REAL ESTATE MARKETING & LEADS</strong><p>See how LeadsFlo connects buyer and seller campaigns, funnels and follow-up workflows.</p><a href="/real-estate-lead-generation/">Explore the Real Estate landing page ↗</a></div>
<section><h2>Start with an eligible housing campaign</h2><p>Ads for residential real estate generally fall under Meta&#x27;s housing Special Ad Category, which restricts certain audience targeting options. Check the platform&#x27;s current housing requirements before launch, including geographic and demographic limitations.</p><p>Fair housing obligations matter beyond platform settings. Ads should not imply preferences or exclusions based on protected characteristics, and the listing broker must authorize the content being promoted.</p></section>
<section><h2>Choose an appropriate campaign objective</h2><p>A listing promotion may aim to generate property inquiries, whereas a seller campaign might introduce a consultation about the local market. Align the destination, form and follow-up process with the specific objective rather than using identical creative for every audience.</p><p>Lead forms can reduce friction, but a lower-friction submission does not automatically mean the contact is interested or ready. Compare actual contact and appointment outcomes.</p></section>
<section><h2>Use accurate, permissioned creative</h2><p>Images and videos should reflect the actual property, area or service being promoted. Avoid invented property details, misleading price claims and unauthorized listing photography. A short walkthrough, agent explanation or practical market update may be suitable depending on permissions.</p><p>When a property changes status or price, update or pause the promotion. The ad should not advertise unavailable inventory as though it were active.</p></section>
<section><h2>Keep the landing page focused</h2><p>A relevant landing page should restate the advertised property or seller offer, show credible agent or brokerage information and provide a clear contact route. It should not require a lengthy form just to receive basic details.</p><p>Use appropriate consent language and avoid collecting more personal information than necessary. A clear explanation of the next step can help people recognize the follow-up.</p></section>
<section><h2>Connect every inquiry to an agent</h2><p>The CRM should capture the source, lead type, property or geographic interest and assignment owner. A timely acknowledgement is helpful, but it should be followed by a genuine human response when appropriate.</p><p>For multi-agent teams, avoid assigning every contact to a generic inbox without accountability. A defined routing policy reduces duplicate outreach and forgotten inquiries.</p></section>
<section><h2>Test for meaningful downstream results</h2><p>Review creative response, inquiries, reached contacts, qualified conversations and confirmed appointments separately. A form with many cheap submissions may perform worse than a more focused campaign that generates fewer but more relevant conversations.</p><p>Make adjustments based on observed results, fair housing compliance and changes in available listings. Avoid interpreting initial form counts as guaranteed sales opportunities.</p></section>
